# HOW LONG A CLIENT MAY TAKE TO SEND ITS REQUEST (HD-182 review). Caddy defaults every
# timeout in this block to 0, i.e. no bound, so without these lines a client that trickles
# a body one byte at a time holds a connection here — and, behind it, one of the permits
# of app.expensive-read.max-in-flight, which is taken before the body is read. As many
# such requests as that share allows (six on the default pool of 10, fewer on a smaller
# one) are the whole expensive-read surface, for the price of one socket each.
#
# THIS FILE IS NOT THE PROTECTION, IT IS THE OUTERMOST LAYER OF IT. Underneath it are two
# bounds that every deployment gets, and only one of them ends a trickled request: the
# permit watchdog (PerPrincipalInFlightLimit.sweepStalePermits), which takes the permit
# back at a fixed age however slowly the client is sending. The other in-app layer,
# TomcatUploadTimeoutCustomizer, pins the gap between two reads of a body at 20 s — a
# TIGHTENING of Tomcat's default, which does not remove the read timeout for the body but
# leaves it at the connector's connectionTimeout (60 s by default, and operator-settable).
# It raises the packet rate an attacker has to sustain; it does not end the hold.
# AND THIS IS ONE OF THE TWO FILES A DEPLOY NEVER SYNCS: like .env, the Caddyfile is
# skipped by ops/deploy/apply-config.sh (HD-199), so an existing box does NOT get these
# lines from a git pull — copy them by hand and reload Caddy.
#
# read_body is an ABSOLUTE deadline for reading the whole request, unlike Tomcat's
# per-read one — a deadline ends a trickle where a gap ceiling only prices it, and that is
# also why this number has to be loose where the other is tight. It has to leave room for
# the largest legal upload over a slow link:
# 5m is 25 MB at ~0.7 Mbit/s. Lower it if your users are all on good links; raising it
# weakens only this layer.
#
# THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO `write` TIMEOUT, and that omission is load-bearing rather than
# forgotten. A slow READER holds a permit the same way a slow sender does, and a write
# deadline is the instrument for it — but Caddy applies one per response, and this
# application serves SSE streams that legitimately live for up to 30 minutes
# (SseRegistry.EMITTER_TIMEOUT_MS). Any value short enough to bound a slow reader would
# cut every notification stream in the product. The permit watchdog is what bounds that
# half, in the one place that can tell a stream from a stall.
{
    servers {
        timeouts {
            read_body 5m
        }
    }
}

# SITE_ADDRESS is injected from .env (see .env.prod.example). Its host(s) must
# match the host in APP_BASE_URL. Example: "example.com, www.example.com".
{$SITE_ADDRESS} {
    # THE UPLOAD CEILING, ENFORCED AT THE EDGE — and this is the only place it can be
    # enforced before the bytes are spent (HD-191).
    #
    # Spring resolves multipart EAGERLY: DispatcherServlet.checkMultipart parses the request
    # before getHandler(), so Tomcat has already streamed the part to a temp file by the time
    # any interceptor, any budget or any Hamstrack code sees it. A body over
    # ATTACHMENT_MAX_UPLOAD_SIZE therefore costs the app a full socket read plus a temp-file
    # write and then answers 413 from the exception handler — having spent no write unit and
    # no upload byte, because no handler was ever mapped. In-process that lane cannot be made
    # to cost anything (the app's own controls run after the parse); here it costs the sender
    # a connection and the app nothing at all.
    #
    # TIED TO THE SAME VARIABLE THE SERVLET CEILING READS
    # (spring.servlet.multipart.max-request-size, application.properties). One number, set in
    # one place, so raising the app limit without raising the proxy limit — the standing
    # mistake this pairing exists to prevent — is not expressible in .env.
    #
    # THE UNITS DIFFER AND THE DIRECTION IS DELIBERATE. Caddy reads `MB` as 10^6 bytes and
    # Spring reads it as 2^20, so `25MB` is 25,000,000 here and 26,214,400 in the app: the
    # edge is ~5% TIGHTER than the servlet ceiling, which is the safe direction — nothing the
    # servlet would have accepted is refused here first by more than that margin, and nothing
    # the servlet would refuse reaches it. Keep ATTACHMENT_MAX_FILE_SIZE comfortably below
    # ATTACHMENT_MAX_UPLOAD_SIZE (the shipped defaults leave 20MB against 25MB) or write the
    # value as a plain byte count, which is the one spelling Caddy and Spring read identically.
    request_body {
        max_size {$ATTACHMENT_MAX_UPLOAD_SIZE:25MB}
    }
    reverse_proxy app:8080
}
